id: 0d00fe0a-7d41-4f3d-8c2c-908792099016
title: Acromegaly
---

# Acromegaly

- **Definition** - caused by growth hormone secretion from a pituitary adenoma, and carries an approximate 2-fold excess mortality when untreated
- **Clinical features of acromegaly** - most common complaints are headache and sweating:
  - **Features related to growth hormone excess:**
    - <u>Gigantism</u> - tall stature if GH hypersecretion occurs before puberty
    - <u>Headache</u> - often severe and disabling, and not fully explained by size of tumour itself (mechanism unclear)
    - <u>Sweating</u> - alterations in sweat gland structure and function (may be irreversible)
    - <u>Enlargement of hands</u> - spade hands which is often related to a change in ring size
    - <u>Enlargement of feet or increased heel pad thickness</u> - due to oedema and accumulation of soft tissue, manifestation as a change in shoe size
    - <u>Proximal muscle weakness</u> - due to myopathy
    - <u>Fluid retention</u> - manifests as pitting oedema and carpal tunnel syndrome
  - **Features related to pituitary tumour:**
    - <u>Visual field deficit</u> - classical abnormality a/w wcompression of the optic chiasm are bitemporal hemianopia (suprasellar extension of tumour), but can also manifest as unilateral loss of acuity, scotoma, or homonymous haemianopia
    - <u>Headache</u> - non-specific, and may be a consequence of stretching of the diraphragma sellae
    - <u>Disconnect hyperprolactinaemia</u> - manifests as galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea and hypogonadism
    - <u>Diplopia</u> - lateral extension int carvenous sinus
- **Complications and comorbidities a/w acromegaly:**
  - <u>Cardiac</u> - HTN, cardiomyopathy, cardiovascular disease (2-3x)
  - <u>Respiratory/ ENT</u> - obstructive sleep apnoea
  - <u>Metabolic</u> - impaired glucose tolerance (25%) or frank T2DM (10%)
  - <u>Gastrointestinal</u> - increased risk of colonic carcinoma (2-3x)
- **Signs of acromegaly:** y
![](../img/clinical_features_of_acromegaly.png)
- **Salient points of Hx** (notes from bedside) - typically do not present w/ coarsening of facial features:
  - Presenting Sx typically of headache, hypogonadism, recent poor control of HTN, DM requiring escalation of medications
  - Subsequent questioning reveals change in ring sizes or shoe sizes
  - Additional query on other features of pituitary tumour (mass effect, hypopituitarism esp. hypogonadism)
  - Hx of cardiovascular disease (due to increased risk)
  - Hx of colorectal carcinoma or recent screening CLN
- **Ix:**
  - <u>IGF-1 screening</u> - elevated serum IGF-1
  - <u>GH suppression test</u> (75g OGTT) - demonstrates failed suppression of GH (N \< 0.5 microgram/L), and in 30% there is a **paradoxical rise**
  - <u>Serum prolactin</u> - elevated in 30% due to disconnect hyperprolactinaemia or a co-secretory tumour
  - <u>Screening CLN</u> - for colonic neoplasms
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>1st line therapy</u> - surgery
    - <u>2nd line therapy</u> - medical and radiotherapy
  - **Surgery** - transphenoidal resection offers a cure in GH excess especially in patients w/ microadenomas
  - **Radiotherapy** - external RT:
    - <u>Indications</u>:
      - Non-resectable tumour
      - Acromegaly persists after surgery (raised GH and IGF-1)
    - <u>S/E</u> - risk of hypopituitarism
  - **Medical therapy:**
    - <u>Selection</u>:
      - Somatostatin analogues - e.g. octreotide, lanreotide (injections every week)
      - GHrH antagonists - pegvisomant (daily injections)
      - Dopamine agonists (less effective but can be considered if concurrent hyperprolactinaemia) - e.g. bromocriptine, carbegoline, quinagolide
    - <u>Indications</u>:
      - Primary therapy - modest tumour shrinkage prior to surgery, or as alternative to surgery
      - Secondary therapy - if surgery failed to normalise GH and IGF-1, and RT and medical therapy considered
    - <u>Dosing</u> - titrate to GH \< 1.5 microgram/L (on OGTT) and normalise serum IGF-1
